THE ABSOLUTE FINAL JARVI MASTER ROADMAP
Here is the micro-optimized, production-ready blueprint.
Phase 1: The Daemon Core & Minimum Viable Income (MVI)
Goal: Establish a crash-resistant, event-driven background service that autonomously generates validated, ready-to-publish income assets.
Daemon Infrastructure:
Implement a lightweight, SQLite-backed Task Queue (e.g., APScheduler or custom asyncio queue) for persistent, crash-recoverable job management.
Integrate the Resource Watchdog and Token Ledger as background daemon threads monitoring the queue and enforcing strict execution timeouts.
The 5-Agent MVI Pipeline (Event-Driven Flow):
Trend Spotter: Identifies 3-5 high-demand, low-competition niches (strict JSON output).
Analyst: Validates niches against ethical/supply-chain rules, outputs a strict Product Requirements Document (PRD).
Scout: Uses foundational scraping (playwright/scrapling) to find exact product links, prices, and ASINs.
Creator: Drafts SEO-optimized copy, affiliate links, and basic media asset prompts.
Publisher: Formats the final payload and pushes it to a local SQLite "Approval Queue" (or watched pending/ directory), requiring only a 5-second Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) review.
State & Checkpointing: The PipelineFlow orchestrator saves its state to SQLite after every agent completes. If the system crashes or restarts, it resumes exactly where it left off.
Phase 2: Advanced Perception & Scaling
Goal: Upgrade the agents' ability to interact with the complex, anti-bot-heavy modern web without getting banned, maintaining the €0-cost mandate.
AI Browser Integration: Upgrade the Scout Agent to use browser-use (local, open-source mode). Connect it to Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint (http://localhost:11434/v1) to provide human-like pacing, DOM parsing, and native anti-bot evasion, replacing brittle custom selectors.
Heavy-Duty Extraction Fallback: Self-host firecrawl via Docker (if RAM permits) as a fallback for the Analyst Agent to extract clean, structured Markdown from complex, JavaScript-heavy documentation or competitor sites.
Dynamic Tool Routing: Implement a lightweight "Router" model that evaluates the task and only injects the necessary tools into the agent's context, reducing token usage by up to 80%.
Phase 3: The Unified Companion & Secure Memory
Goal: Jarvi interacts with your personal digital life, leveraging the business infrastructure while maintaining absolute, uncompromising security boundaries.
Strict Tool-Level & Data Isolation: Personal data (emails, calendar) is stored in a strictly isolated, encrypted local Vector DB. Crucially, the business agents' ToolRegistry physically does not contain the definitions for personal tools. Even if a prompt is compromised, the business agent cannot call them.
Secure API Integration: Implement OAuth2 flows for Google/Microsoft. Build "Tools" for the Companion LLM: read_calendar(), draft_email().
Unified (Read-Only) RAG: The Companion can query the business Vector DB (read-only) to answer questions like "How did our affiliate links perform this week?", but business agents cannot query the personal DB.
Mobile Access: Expose the local FastAPI backend securely to your phone via Tailscale or Cloudflare Tunnels (zero router port forwarding).
Phase 4: Agentic Self-Improvement & MCP Readiness
Goal: Jarvi writes, tests, and maintains its own code, evolving into a true autonomous entity.
MCP-Ready Tool Registry: Refactor all agent tools (e.g., search_web, backup_workspace) to follow the Model Context Protocol (MCP) schema, making them instantly compatible with any MCP client.
Sandboxed Code Generation: Jarvi uses qwen2.5-coder to write new Python scripts for new tasks, executing them only in isolated Docker containers to prevent host system damage.
Automated Testing: Jarvi writes pytest unit tests for its own code. The Resource Watchdog is upgraded to monitor the test suite, ensuring no new code degrades system performance.